# Drafted by Gemini (R11 batch b6), edited. jeremiah_stover's first conversation.

~ start
set jeremiah_stover_met
Narrator: Damp black loam clings to his boots, and his knuckles are split from cold dirt and hard words.
jeremiah_stover [weary]: Mind where you tread, strangers. A man works twelve hours in the clod, and the Mayor still reckons a sack of pale tubers is worth three copper less than last moon.
-> menu

~ menu
* Ask about his work with the farmers -> work
* Ask what ails the harvest in Foxwillow -> foxwillow
* Ask what he makes of Wickermoor Village -> village
* [Persuasion DC 13] Offer a hand with his union trouble -> union_ok | union_fail
* Take our leave -> END

~ work
jeremiah_stover [angry]: I speak for every spade and plough round this moor. Without our roots in the pot, Wickermoor starves, yet Somerton pays us in prayers to his oak.
-> menu

~ foxwillow
jeremiah_stover [sad]: The pumpkin-headed straw folk out Foxwillow way cannot pull a clean crop from the furrow. Something sours the soil, and the union will pay good silver to whoever sets it right.
-> menu

~ village
jeremiah_stover [neutral]: Fine timber and proud lanterns at the Founder's Round, paid for by folk with mud under their nails. Keep your eyes on your purse when the tithe-basket passes.
-> menu

~ union_ok
jeremiah_stover [smile]: Well now, you have an honest grip to you. Tell the straw folk in Foxwillow that Jeremiah Stover sent you, and they will show you the blighted fields.
-> menu

~ union_fail
jeremiah_stover [weary]: Talk is cheap as chaff, friend. Put your boots in the mire out by Foxwillow first, then we can speak of fair wages.
-> menu
